      "content": "You have correctly identified the most significant debate in American constitutional law: both originalism and substantive due process are inherently value-laden, and neither framework can be objectively proven \"correct\" without prior agreement on foundational political philosophy. While the majority claims originalism as an objective good for stability, critics argue it can be used selectively to achieve political outcomes they favor rather than strictly adhering to historical facts.\n\nThe rule of law favors a consistent methodology to prevent arbitrary judicial power, which is why legal education strongly emphasizes understanding the *process* of interpretation (like self-consistency and precedent) over the truth of the *outcome*. Ultimately, whether one side is \"right\" depends less on legal logic and more on one's underlying moral beliefs regarding the proper role of government and the judiciary.",
